Comments: [Evaluator: Christian Iliadis; Date: 11/19/2024]
1. Er = 946 keV: calculated using the predicted excitation energy in Herndl et al. 1995; their Coulomb shift calculations reproduce the experimental first-excited
   state energy in 28S, and the first- and second-excited state energies in the 28Mg mirror within 50 keV; the large uncertainty in the resonance energy derives
   mainly from that of the Q value.
2. Er = 1476 and 1860 keV: the resonance energies are equal to the beta-delayed proton decay energies measured in Gillespie et al. 2022.
3. The proton widths of the resonances are estimated using the shell-model spectroscopic factors of Herndl et al. 1995 and Gillespie et al. 2022.
4. The gamma-ray partial widths of the resonances are averages of the shell-model results of Herndl et al. 1995 and the values calculated from the measured 
   lifetimes of the 28Mg mirror states.
5. The direct capture S factor is estimated using the shell-model spectroscopic factors of Herndl et al. 1995.
